            concept: "A lock-free stack pushes and pops by CAS-ing the head pointer in a retry loop: read the head, link the new node to it, and CAS the head to the new node. If another thread changed the head first, the CAS fails and the thread retries.",
            syntax: "do { old = head; node->next = old; } while (!CAS(&head, old, node));",
            exampleCode: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(void) {\n    int values[] = {10, 15, 3, 8, 13};\n    int max = values[0];\n    for (int i = 1; i < 5; i++) if (values[i] > max) max = values[i];\n    printf(\"max: %d\\n\", max);\n    return 0;\n}\n",
            exampleLanguage: "c",
            mistake: "Freeing a popped node immediately, when another thread may still be reading it.",
            bug: { prompt: "The code below opens a file but never closes it. Find and fix the bug.", buggyCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);", fixedCode: "FILE *f = fopen(\"data.txt\", \"r\");\nif (f == NULL) return 1;\nchar line[128];\nfgets(line, sizeof line, f);\nprintf(\"%s\", line);\nfclose(f);", explanation: "Every successful fopen needs a matching fclose, otherwise the file descriptor and buffer leak." },
